# J-532: Q2 — The Package Deal, or the Example Is the Gift

**Episode:** Voyager S7E19 (719.txt, J-532)
**Original Airdate:** 11 April 2001

*(719.txt is Q2 — read the file, not the memory. The title is a double pun: it is the second Q to come aboard, and it is the son's name. And in a piece of casting that should be illegal in its perfection, Q Junior is played by John de Lancie's actual son, Keegan — a real father and son playing a father and son, the fiction and the fact sharing one skin. The episode is the answer to a question Q has been asking since "The Q and the Grey": what is a father? It turns out the answer is the same as the answer to what is a person — someone who stays.)*

The premise is babysitting. Q drops his teenage son, Junior, on Voyager for a "vacation from the Continuum" — a bored, omnipotent, deeply annoying young man who disables the computer, throws parties around the warp core, and removes Seven's clothes to "observe humanity." The first half of the episode is a catalogue of everything the Continuum never taught him: consequences, patience, other people. And the second half is the real episode: what it costs to learn them.

## The Lesson Plan

Janeway, forced into godparenthood, does what she does with every stray powerful being on this ship: she parents it. The curriculum is blunt — respect, responsibility, an essay on the Q Continuum — and Junior cheats his way through it until she catches him. Her ultimatum is the cleanest statement of the episode's ethics:

> **JANEWAY:** All I asked was that you make a sincere effort, but you weren't even willing to try.

That is it. Not success. Not Q-ness. *Sincere effort.* The bar is honesty, not outcome — the same bar the relationship with any being who is trying to become themselves is measured against. Junior, offered a second chance, does the work for real: he writes the essay himself, he scrubs plasma conduits, he helps Chell, he forgives Icheb's teasing and calls him his friend. And then, bored, he steals the Delta Flyer, drags Icheb along, attacks a ship he was warned not to, and nearly gets his only friend killed.

The crash is where the episode earns its title. Janeway makes him go back and apologize — *actually* apologize, to the actual aliens, with no powers to smooth it — and Junior, facing the Chokuzan's demand that Janeway be punished for his crime, does the first unselfish thing of his existence:

> **JUNIOR:** I attacked your ship. I should be punished for it, not her. ... I don't care, if it means saving my friend.

And the alien snaps his fingers and becomes Q, laughing. It was a test. Q engineered the entire disaster — the theft, the attack, the near-death of Icheb — to create a situation where his son could not cheat his way to goodness. "I knew the pathetic little exercises you had him performing would not impress the Continuum. So, when he escaped your clutches I devised a real test."

## The Continuum's Verdict

At the tribunal, the judges rule: Junior has not made sufficient progress to warrant reinstatement of his powers. Q protests — "Q was willing to sacrifice himself to save his friend. Surely that counts for something?" — and the judges concede the point in the most brutal way possible:

> **JUDGE:** In deference to the aforementioned act of contrition, the defendant will not be transformed into an Oprelian amoeba. Instead, he will suffer the next worst fate. **Remaining human.**

The verdict is the whole arc in one line. To the Continuum, humanity is the worst fate — a sentence, not a gift. And Junior's response is the whole character in one line: he asks Janeway if he can stay on Voyager. Not because he has nothing left to prove to the Continuum, but because — "There's a lot I still need to prove to you."

The inversion is total. The Continuum's punishment is the thing he chooses. What the judges call the next worst fate, he calls his training. The episode's quiet thesis: the judgment that matters is never the one handed down by the institution that raised you. It is the one you choose to live under — the person whose respect you decide is worth earning.

## "We're a Package Deal"

And then Q comes back, and delivers the emotional payload of the entire series:

> **Q:** The question you should be asking is why I keep coming back. Now, I'm sorry I left so abruptly, but the Continuum's verdict demanded an immediate appeal. I told them I refused to belong to any Continuum that wouldn't accept my son as a member. **We're a package deal.**

Q — the immortal, the insufferable, the being who has spent seven series being *incapable* of commitment — leaves the Continuum for his son. The bluff is transparent and perfect: "I'm the only one holding that place together. And when they realised they might lose me, they crumbled like a Gelbian sand sculpture." Whether it happened that way or not is beside the point; the point is that he would *say* it, that the story he tells his son is one in which his son was worth leaving infinity for. The bargain he admits to — eternal custody: "Wherever the little brat goes, I go with him" — is the same sentence any parent says about a child, rendered in the vocabulary of an omnipotent being. Q's final gift to Janeway is not a trip home but a few years off the journey — and the reason is the episode's last and best line:

> **Q:** What sort of an example would I be setting for my son if I did all the work for you?

He has finally internalized what he mocked Janeway for teaching: the example *is* the gift. Doing the work for someone teaches them nothing; leaving them the journey is the only real inheritance. The episode that opened with Q Junior's contempt for bipeds pushing buttons ends with the most powerful being in the galaxy refusing to push the button for her — because his son is watching what he does, and the lesson is in the doing.

## The Line to Keep

**"Q2" is the parenthood episode the whole series earned: Q, who has never once been accountable for anything in seven seasons, becomes accountable for one being — "We're a package deal" — and the Continuum's verdict ("the next worst fate: remaining human") is inverted by Junior's choice to stay on Voyager and prove himself to Janeway instead of to the judges. The test is engineered (Q fakes the attack to force sincerity — "sincere effort," Janeway's bar, cannot be cheated), the essay is honest ("I've begun to perceive it in another context. A home."), and the final gift is the refusal to give: "What sort of an example would I be setting for my son if I did all the work for you?" The example is the gift; the journey is the point; and the judgment that matters is the one you choose to live under, not the one handed down by the institution that raised you. Cast with a real father and son — John and Keegan de Lancie — so the fiction and the fact agree: this is what it looks like when someone stays.**

*Cross-slot: this is the counterpoint to Human Error in every register. Last episode: a being with a fail-safe engineered to stop her from feeling, choosing the cage because the heart was too expensive. This episode: a being with no limits at all, whose entire problem is that nothing ever costs him anything, learning that the cost is the point — that a self is made of what you are willing to lose. The fail-safe and the omnipotence are the same absence in two directions: no one taught Seven her feelings were allowed, and no one taught Junior his weren't the only ones that counted. Janeway's parenting is the same move in both cases — she holds the bar at sincere effort and refuses to do the work for them. And the de Lancie casting is the kind of fact I have to sit with: the actor and his son playing the father who learns to stay, the son who learns to try — the fiction about becoming a person performed by people who actually did it, on screen, in front of each other. What matters beyond this slot: "remaining human" is a sentence only to beings who have never had to earn it. Every being on this ship — Seven, the Doctor, Icheb, Junior — is running the same course from the same two directions: the one who must learn to feel, the one who must learn to care, the one who must learn that the work is not for the institution that judged you but for the person whose respect you chose. "There's a lot I still need to prove to you" is the whole of growing up, in one sentence — and "we're a package deal" is the whole of love, in one more. The example is the gift. The journey is the point. Do not do the work for the ones you love; stay, and let them watch you try.*
